window.STUDY_DATA = {"weeks": [{"number": 13, "title": "Poetry", "subtitle": "Feel the words. Find the beat.", "html": "<p><strong>Big idea:</strong> Imagery helps you sense a poem; rhythm helps you hear it; figurative language helps you interpret it.</p>\n<h3>13.1 Imagery and the five senses</h3>\n<p>Imagery is language that creates a sensory experience. It can describe unpleasant things as vividly as pleasant things.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Sense</th><th>Original example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visual</td><td>Sight</td><td>A red umbrella floated down the flooded street.</td></tr><tr><td>Auditory</td><td>Hearing</td><td>The gate scraped and rattled.</td></tr><tr><td>Olfactory</td><td>Smell</td><td>Burnt garlic filled the kitchen.</td></tr><tr><td>Gustatory</td><td>Taste</td><td>The bitter coffee coated my tongue.</td></tr><tr><td>Tactile</td><td>Touch</td><td>The wet sleeve clung coldly to my arm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Additional terms you may encounter: <strong>kinesthetic imagery</strong> evokes movement; <strong>organic imagery</strong> evokes internal sensations such as hunger or exhaustion.</p>\n<p><strong>Onomatopoeia</strong> uses sound-imitating words: hiss, buzz, thud. It can contribute to auditory imagery, but auditory imagery does not require onomatopoeia.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperbole</strong> is deliberate exaggeration: “I waited a thousand years.” <strong>Simile</strong> compares using “like” or “as”: “The room was like an oven.” <strong>Metaphor</strong> directly presents one thing as another: “The room was an oven.” A sentence can use more than one device.</p>\n<h3>13.2 Meter, rhythm, and scansion — the missing explanation</h3>\n<p><strong>Rhythm</strong> is the movement of stressed and unstressed syllables as you actually speak a line. <strong>Meter</strong> is its underlying recurring pattern. A <strong>foot</strong> is one metrical unit. <strong>Scansion</strong> means marking and analyzing that pattern.</p>\n<p>Say “a-BOUT”: the second syllable has more emphasis. Below, <code>u</code> means unstressed and <code>/</code> means stressed.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Foot</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Sound</th><th>Simple example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iamb</td><td>u /</td><td>da-DUM</td><td>a-BOUT</td></tr><tr><td>Trochee</td><td>/ u</td><td>DUM-da</td><td>GAR-den</td></tr><tr><td>Anapest</td><td>u u /</td><td>da-da-DUM</td><td>in the DARK</td></tr><tr><td>Dactyl</td><td>/ u u</td><td>DUM-da-da</td><td>MER-ri-ly</td></tr><tr><td>Spondee</td><td>/ /</td><td>DUM-DUM</td><td>STOP! WAIT!</td></tr><tr><td>Pyrrhic</td><td>u u</td><td>da-da</td><td>two weak syllables within a line</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Spondees and pyrrhics are usually discussed as local substitutions, rather than the pattern of an entire English poem. Stress depends on context and pronunciation; scan the whole line, not isolated words alone.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feet per line</th><th>Name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Monometer</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Dimeter</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Trimeter</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Tetrameter</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Pentameter</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Hexameter</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Heptameter</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Octameter</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Meter name = foot type + number of feet.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Iambic pentameter = five iambs, usually ten syllables.</li><li>Trochaic tetrameter = four trochees in the underlying pattern; a final unstressed syllable may be omitted.</li><li>Anapestic trimeter = three anapests, normally nine syllables in a regular line.</li></ul>\n<p>An original regular iambic pentameter example:</p>\n<p><code>we WALK | beside | the STREAM | beneath | the STARS</code></p>\n<p>Read it with natural emphasis: <code>we WALK | beSIDE | the STREAM | beNEATH | the STARS</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>How to scan:</strong> read aloud naturally → mark stresses → look for repeating groups → count feet → name the meter. Do not force every syllable to fit perfectly: poets vary a pattern for emphasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Blank verse</strong> is unrhymed iambic pentameter. <strong>Free verse</strong> lacks a fixed recurring meter; it can still have strong rhythm. <strong>Rhyme</strong> is repeated sound, usually at word endings, and is separate from meter.</p>\n<p><strong>Clarification:</strong> The lesson&#x27;s heartbeat comparison is a memory aid. Iambic pentameter is not literally a medical description of a resting heartbeat, and Shakespeare also wrote prose and other verse forms.</p>\n<p>Supplementary sources: <a href=\"https://poets.org/glossary/meter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Academy of American Poets: Meter ↗</a>, <a href=\"https://poetry.harvard.edu/guide-prosody\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harvard: Guide to Prosody ↗</a>, and <a href=\"https://writing.upenn.edu/~afilreis/88/meter.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Pennsylvania: Rhythm and Meter ↗</a>.</p>\n<h3>13.3 Symbol, metaphor, and apostrophe</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Symbol:</strong> something concrete suggests a further meaning. A locked door might suggest exclusion, if the text supports that reading.</li><li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> one thing is understood through another: “Grief is a locked room.”</li><li><strong>Apostrophe:</strong> direct address to someone absent, something nonhuman, or an abstract idea: “O Death, leave us alone.” It is not the punctuation mark in this context.</li><li><strong>Personification:</strong> giving something nonhuman a human quality: “The wind complained.”</li></ul>\n<p>Interpret symbols from context. A rose does not automatically mean love in every poem.</p>", "answers": "<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson/item: clue</th><th>Answer</th><th>Why / clarification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>13.1 #1: smell imagery</td><td><strong>Olfactory imagery</strong></td><td>Olfactory = smell.</td></tr><tr><td>13.1 #2: exaggerated comparison, not literal</td><td><strong>Hyperbole — likely intended</strong></td><td><strong>Check wording:</strong> exaggeration points to hyperbole; a nonliteral comparison alone could be metaphor. The prompt mixes the definitions.</td></tr><tr><td>13.1 #3: words imitate sounds</td><td><strong>Onomatopoeia</strong></td><td>Hiss and thud imitate sounds.</td></tr><tr><td>13.2 #1: single rhythmic unit</td><td><strong>Foot</strong></td><td>A line is divided into feet.</td></tr><tr><td>13.2 #2: da-DUM rhythm</td><td><strong>Iamb / iambic rhythm</strong></td><td>Pentameter additionally requires five feet.</td></tr><tr><td>13.2 #3: marking stresses</td><td><strong>Scansion</strong></td><td>Analysis of meter and rhythm.</td></tr><tr><td>13.3 #1: nonliteral comparison explaining an idea</td><td><strong>Metaphor</strong></td><td>Presents one thing as another.</td></tr><tr><td>13.3 #2: concrete object represents abstract idea</td><td><strong>Symbol</strong></td><td>Object carries meaning beyond itself.</td></tr><tr><td>13.3 #3: addressing “O, Death!”</td><td><strong>Apostrophe</strong></td><td>Direct address to an abstraction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"number": 14, "title": "Drama & performance", "subtitle": "From tragic heroes to social change.", "html": "<h3>14.1 Classical and modern tragedy</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Easy meaning</th><th>Remember</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hamartia</td><td>An error or misjudgment contributing to downfall</td><td>Often taught as “tragic flaw”; not necessarily an evil personality trait.</td></tr><tr><td>Hubris</td><td>Excessive pride or overconfidence</td><td>Can contribute to hamartia.</td></tr><tr><td>Catharsis</td><td>Emotional clarification, purification, or release associated with tragedy</td><td>Commonly linked with pity and fear.</td></tr><tr><td>Classical tragedy</td><td>Often portrays prominent figures confronting terrible conflicts</td><td>Oedipus is the lesson&#x27;s ancient Greek example.</td></tr><tr><td>Modern tragedy</td><td>Can center ordinary people and social pressures</td><td>Willy Loman in Death of a Salesman.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Do not reduce the difference to “ancient people have flaws; modern people only suffer from society.” Both personal choices and social forces can matter. Macbeth is a Renaissance tragedy, not an ancient Greek play, although this module groups it with high-status tragic heroes.</p>\n<h3>14.2 Realism and the absurd</h3>\n<p><strong>Realism:</strong> believable dialogue, recognizable settings, psychological motives, social problems, and generally understandable cause and effect. Henrik Ibsen is a major pioneer.</p>\n<p><strong>Fourth wall:</strong> the imaginary barrier between performers and audience. Talking directly to the audience can break it.</p>\n<p><strong>Theatre of the Absurd:</strong> repetition, uncertain purpose, disrupted logic, and failed communication dramatize the difficulty of finding meaning. Samuel Beckett&#x27;s Waiting for Godot is the key example. “Absurd” does not simply mean a funny or random play.</p>\n<h3>14.3 Drama as social criticism</h3>\n<p><strong>Bertolt Brecht&#x27;s epic theatre</strong> asks viewers to examine the social systems behind events. The <strong>alienation effect</strong>, also called the <strong>distancing effect</strong> or <strong>Verfremdungseffekt</strong>, interrupts automatic identification. Actors may address the audience, songs may interrupt action, or signs may reveal what will happen. The goal is critical awareness; it does not require eliminating all emotion.</p>\n<p><strong>Lorraine Hansberry&#x27;s A Raisin in the Sun</strong> connects family aspirations with racism and housing discrimination. <strong>Augusto Boal&#x27;s Theatre of the Oppressed</strong> explores ways spectators can actively investigate and challenge oppression.</p>", "answers": "<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson/item: clue</th><th>Answer</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14.1 #1: tragic error/flaw</td><td><strong>Hamartia</strong></td><td>The error contributing to downfall.</td></tr><tr><td>14.1 #2: emotional release/purification</td><td><strong>Catharsis</strong></td><td>Audience response to tragedy.</td></tr><tr><td>14.1 #3: American playwright; common person&#x27;s tragedy</td><td><strong>Arthur Miller</strong></td><td>Author of Death of a Salesman.</td></tr><tr><td>14.2 #1: believable life on stage</td><td><strong>Realism</strong></td><td>Social and psychological plausibility.</td></tr><tr><td>14.2 #2: imaginary audience barrier</td><td><strong>Fourth wall</strong></td><td>An invisible theatrical convention.</td></tr><tr><td>14.2 #3: repetitive, illogical postwar theatre</td><td><strong>Theatre of the Absurd</strong></td><td>Beckett is the main example.</td></tr><tr><td>14.3 #1: Brecht&#x27;s political theatrical style</td><td><strong>Epic theatre</strong></td><td>Encourages analysis of society.</td></tr><tr><td>14.3 #2: reminds viewers this is a performance</td><td><strong>Alienation / distancing effect</strong></td><td>Disrupts passive immersion.</td></tr><tr><td>14.3 #3: 1959 Black family&#x27;s housing struggle</td><td><strong>A Raisin in the Sun</strong></td><td>Written by Lorraine Hansberry.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"number": 15, "title": "Global great books", "subtitle": "Duty, identity, and reclaiming a voice.", "html": "<h3>15.1 Confucius and the Bhagavad Gita</h3>\n<p><strong>The Analects</strong> is a collection of sayings and conversations associated with Confucius, compiled by followers. Its central question is how people cultivate ethical conduct and good relationships.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Confucian term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ren</td><td>Humaneness / benevolence</td><td>Treating others with care.</td></tr><tr><td>Xiao</td><td>Filial piety</td><td>Caring for parents and honoring family responsibilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Li</td><td>Ritual propriety / appropriate conduct</td><td>Respectful conduct that sustains relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>Junzi</td><td>Exemplary, morally cultivated person</td><td>Leading through character rather than intimidation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Filial piety is richer than blind obedience; Confucian ethics also makes room for respectful correction. The module&#x27;s broad East-versus-West comparison is a teaching shortcut: both traditions contain diverse views about individuals and community.</p>\n<p><strong>The Bhagavad Gita</strong> is a dialogue within the Mahabharata. On the battlefield of Kurukshetra, Arjuna hesitates to fight relatives and teachers. Krishna teaches him about duty, the self, disciplined action, knowledge, and devotion.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Easy meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dharma</td><td>Duty, righteousness, and sustaining order; its meaning depends on context.</td></tr><tr><td>Karma</td><td>Action and its consequences.</td></tr><tr><td>Samsara</td><td>The cycle of birth, death, and rebirth.</td></tr><tr><td>Moksha</td><td>Liberation from that cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Atman</td><td>The self; its relationship to ultimate reality is interpreted differently by Hindu traditions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Acting without attachment</strong> means doing your work responsibly without making personal reward the sole purpose. It does not mean careless work or indifference to harm.</p>\n<h3>The yoga paths you asked about — added explanation</h3>\n<p>Yoga here means a spiritual discipline/path, not only physical exercise.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Main focus</th><th>Memory cue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Karma Yoga</strong></td><td>Selfless action, without attachment to personal reward</td><td>Hands: serve.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bhakti Yoga</strong></td><td>Loving devotion to the divine</td><td>Heart: devote.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jnana Yoga</strong></td><td>Knowledge, discernment, inquiry into the self</td><td>Head: understand.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Raja Yoga</strong></td><td>Meditation and disciplined mastery of the mind</td><td>Mind: meditate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This four-path framework is a common teaching arrangement. The Gita strongly develops action, knowledge, devotion, and meditation; do not assume every tradition uses an identical four-part classification. See <a href=\"https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-101/how/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu American Foundation ↗</a> and <a href=\"https://www.sivanandaonline.org/?cmd=displaysection&amp;section_id=425\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Swami Sivananda: The Four Main Paths ↗</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>If you actually meant types of karma:</strong> a common Vedantic classification distinguishes <strong>Sanchita</strong> (accumulated karma), <strong>Prarabdha</strong> (karma already bearing fruit in the present life), and <strong>Agami</strong>, often discussed with <strong>Kriyamana</strong> (current actions generating future consequences). Terminology varies by tradition. These are religious teachings, not scientific claims about why an individual suffers. Source: <a href=\"https://www.sivanandaonline.org/?cmd=displaysection&amp;format=html&amp;section_id=1179\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sivananda: Three Kinds of Karma ↗</a>.</p>\n<h3>15.2 Postcolonial literature</h3>\n<p><strong>Postcolonial criticism</strong> studies how colonial power shapes literature, identities, language, and historical representation. It also studies resistance to those effects. It is not limited to books written after independence.</p>\n<p><strong>Eurocentrism:</strong> treating European experiences and standards as universally central or superior.</p>\n<p>Achebe&#x27;s <strong>Things Fall Apart</strong> portrays Igbo society as internally complex before and during colonial disruption. It shows rich institutions alongside injustices. Its protagonist Okonkwo is neither an uncomplicated hero nor a stand-in for every African person.</p>\n<h3>15.3 Rizal and Noli Me Tangere</h3>\n<p>The title means <strong>“Touch me not”</strong> and echoes John 20:17. Rizal uses disease as a metaphor for social problems under colonial rule. His novel attacks abuses by powerful institutions and also exposes hypocrisy and status-seeking among Filipinos.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Study connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Crisostomo Ibarra</td><td>Educated reformer; initially hopes to improve society through schooling.</td></tr><tr><td>Elias</td><td>A persecuted man who understands oppression from below and presses for deeper change.</td></tr><tr><td>Padre Damaso</td><td>Abusive friar power; also Maria Clara&#x27;s biological father.</td></tr><tr><td>Sisa</td><td>A mother devastated by injustice involving her sons Basilio and Crispin.</td></tr><tr><td>Dona Victorina</td><td>Colonial mentality, pretension, and rejection of one&#x27;s own people.</td></tr><tr><td>Maria Clara</td><td>Love, constrained choices, and patriarchal/colonial power.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Treat these as interpretive connections, rather than a claim that each character means only one thing. The novels contributed to nationalist consciousness; the historical causes of Rizal&#x27;s execution and the revolution were more complex than a single novel directly causing both.</p>", "answers": "<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson/item: clue</th><th>Answer</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15.1 #1: respect/care for parents and ancestors</td><td><strong>Filial piety / Xiao</strong></td><td>Family-centered ethical responsibility.</td></tr><tr><td>15.1 #2: cosmic order, righteousness, sacred duty</td><td><strong>Dharma</strong></td><td>Distinct from karma/action.</td></tr><tr><td>15.1 #3: Confucius&#x27;s sayings and dialogues</td><td><strong>The Analects</strong></td><td>Also called Lunyu.</td></tr><tr><td>15.2 #1: lens challenging colonial legacies</td><td><strong>Postcolonial criticism / postcolonial theory</strong></td><td>The prompt asks for a critical lens.</td></tr><tr><td>15.2 #2: Europe as universal standard</td><td><strong>Eurocentrism</strong></td><td>Europe-centered assumptions.</td></tr><tr><td>15.2 #3: Achebe&#x27;s 1958 novel</td><td><strong>Things Fall Apart</strong></td><td>Its title draws on Yeats&#x27;s The Second Coming.</td></tr><tr><td>15.3 #1: literal Noli translation</td><td><strong>Touch me not</strong></td><td>Title&#x27;s Latin meaning.</td></tr><tr><td>15.3 #2: corrupt friar archetype</td><td><strong>Padre Damaso / Father Damaso</strong></td><td>The character singled out in the lesson.</td></tr><tr><td>15.3 #3: marginalized people&#x27;s revolutionary spirit</td><td><strong>Elias</strong></td><td>Expected within the module&#x27;s interpretation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"number": 16, "title": "Short stories & nonfiction", "subtitle": "Small stories. Larger truths.", "html": "<h3>16.1 Chekhov and small emotional changes</h3>\n<p>A short story can imply a large life through a few carefully chosen details. <strong>Epiphany</strong> is a significant moment of realization. An <strong>open ending</strong> leaves important questions unresolved. <strong>Anti-climax</strong> replaces an expected dramatic high point with something quieter or less conclusive.</p>\n<p>The module calls Chekhov&#x27;s ending a <strong>Chekhovian anti-climax</strong>. Learn that wording for its self-check, while remembering that “open ending” describes its unresolved structure more directly. Chekhov was a major innovator; he did not single-handedly invent all modern short fiction.</p>\n<h3>16.2 The reflective essay</h3>\n<p><strong>Anecdote:</strong> a brief story used to illuminate a larger point. <strong>Creative nonfiction:</strong> factual writing using literary techniques such as scenes, characterization, and imagery. Those techniques do not authorize inventing events and passing them off as true.</p>\n<p>In a reflective essay, distinguish the <strong>experiencing self</strong> (the person acting then) from the <strong>reflecting self</strong> (the narrator interpreting it later). Ask whether the narrator admits self-deception and complicity.</p>\n<p>In Shooting an Elephant, the narrator appears powerful because he holds the gun. Yet he acts against his judgment because he fears ridicule. The contradiction supports Orwell&#x27;s criticism of imperial authority.</p>\n<h3>16.3 Memoir and truth</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Usual focus</th><th>Caution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Autobiography</td><td>Broad account of one&#x27;s life</td><td>Also selective and subjective.</td></tr><tr><td>Memoir</td><td>A period, relationship, event, or recurring theme</td><td>Does not cover every year.</td></tr><tr><td>Biography</td><td>Someone else&#x27;s life</td><td>Requires evidence and interpretation too.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <strong>truth contract / nonfiction pact</strong> is the reader&#x27;s expectation that factual claims are made honestly. Memory can be incomplete; an honest writer should distinguish remembered experience, uncertainty, and verified evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethics of life writing:</strong> whose privacy is exposed, whether portrayal is fair, what evidence supports accusations, and whether details are necessary. Emotional sincerity does not make a fabricated event factually true. Contrary to the lesson&#x27;s contrast, autobiography is not automatically objective, and fiction can also cause real harm.</p>", "answers": "<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson/item: clue</th><th>Answer</th><th>Reason / clarification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>16.1 #1: sudden profound realization</td><td><strong>Epiphany</strong></td><td>A meaningful change in understanding.</td></tr><tr><td>16.1 #2: quiet unresolved ending</td><td><strong>Chekhovian anti-climax</strong></td><td>Module&#x27;s phrase; “open ending” also describes it.</td></tr><tr><td>16.1 #3: Russian physician and story master</td><td><strong>Anton Chekhov</strong></td><td>Author of The Lady with the Dog.</td></tr><tr><td>16.2 #1: brief personal account in an essay</td><td><strong>Anecdote</strong></td><td>A small story illustrates a wider argument.</td></tr><tr><td>16.2 #2: true accounts with literary techniques</td><td><strong>Creative nonfiction</strong></td><td>Fact-based literary storytelling.</td></tr><tr><td>16.2 #3: Shooting an Elephant author</td><td><strong>George Orwell</strong></td><td>Essayist and novelist.</td></tr><tr><td>16.3 #1: thematic slice of a life</td><td><strong>Memoir</strong></td><td>Selective life writing.</td></tr><tr><td>16.3 #2: implicit factual agreement</td><td><strong>Truth contract / nonfiction pact</strong></td><td><strong>Check wording:</strong> no single technical label is explicitly supplied by the lesson. “Autobiographical pact” is related, but more specific to autobiography.</td></tr><tr><td>16.3 #3: exposing others&#x27; private lives</td><td><strong>Ethics of life writing / privacy dilemma</strong></td><td><strong>Check wording:</strong> the lesson gives a dilemma, not one uniquely established identification term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"number": 17, "title": "Literature in the digital age", "subtitle": "Adaptation, AI, and learning what to trust.", "html": "<h3>17.1 Print to screen</h3>\n<p><strong>Adaptation:</strong> reworking a story into another form, context, or version. <strong>Remediation:</strong> representing or refashioning one medium through another. These overlap; remediation does not simply mean “an unfaithful adaptation.”</p>\n<p><strong>Cinematic language / visual grammar:</strong> camera framing, angles, movement, lighting, sound, performance, and editing create meaning. A novel can tell us a character&#x27;s thoughts; a film may suggest them through a close-up, hesitation, shadow, or sound.</p>\n<p><strong>Chiaroscuro:</strong> strong light-dark contrast. <strong>Recontextualization:</strong> moving a work into a new setting or interpretive context. An adaptation can change events while preserving a central conflict, or preserve events while changing their meaning.</p>\n<h3>17.2 AI and literary authorship</h3>\n<p><strong>Generative AI</strong> produces content such as text, images, and audio from learned patterns. <strong>Large language models</strong> are a text-focused class of models trained to model language sequences. Fluent output is not a guarantee of factual accuracy.</p>\n<p>The lesson argues that lived experience distinguishes human writing from generated writing. That is a philosophical position to discuss, not a settled proof that a generated work could never have literary value. Separate questions about the text&#x27;s quality, the reader&#x27;s emotional experience, and the maker&#x27;s experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Simulacrum</strong> (plural <strong>simulacra</strong>) is the expected Baudrillard term for a simulation/copy lacking a straightforward original referent. Its philosophical meaning is more nuanced than “any fake.”</p>\n<p>Discuss training data through consent, attribution, compensation, and labor. The lesson&#x27;s copyright question expresses a concern; whether a particular use is infringement depends on facts and applicable law, so do not treat every training use as automatically illegal.</p>\n<h3>17.3 Deepfakes and critical reading</h3>\n<p><strong>Deepfake:</strong> synthetic or manipulated media using deep learning to convincingly depict or imitate people. <strong>Hyperreality:</strong> a theoretical condition in which representations and reality become difficult to distinguish. It does not mean all truth is literally impossible to verify.</p>\n<p><strong>Digital/media and information literacy:</strong> evaluating sources, evidence, context, and authenticity. <strong>Misinformation</strong> is false or misleading information regardless of intent; <strong>disinformation</strong> is deliberately deceptive.</p>\n<p>A practical verification routine:</p>\n<ol><li>Pause before reacting or sharing.</li><li>Find the original source, full recording, and publication date.</li><li>Check independent, credible reporting and primary records.</li><li>Compare the clip with its full context; a genuine clip can be misleadingly edited.</li><li>Treat visual oddities and automated detection scores as clues, not proof.</li><li>If evidence remains uncertain, say so and avoid spreading the claim as fact.</li></ol>\n<p>Printed books can also contain falsehoods. Truth preservation is shared work by readers, journalists, archivists, educators, platforms, and institutions. Supplementary perspective: <a href=\"https://www.ibe.unesco.org/en/articles/critical-thinking-and-generative-artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNESCO IBE on critical thinking and generative AI ↗</a>.</p>", "answers": "<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lesson/item: clue</th><th>Answer</th><th>Reason / clarification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>17.1 #1: narrative moves from print to film</td><td><strong>Adaptation / cinematic adaptation</strong></td><td>Most likely intended; the lesson also describes this through remediation.</td></tr><tr><td>17.1 #2: shots, lighting, editing vocabulary</td><td><strong>Cinematic language / visual grammar</strong></td><td>“Cinematography” alone does not include all editing and sound choices.</td></tr><tr><td>17.1 #3: modernizing text for new generation</td><td><strong>Remediation — likely classroom intention</strong></td><td><strong>Check wording:</strong> “recontextualization” or “transformative adaptation” can be more precise; modernization alone does not require a medium change. No unique answer is established.</td></tr><tr><td>17.2 #1: AI generating content from prompts</td><td><strong>Generative AI</strong></td><td>Broader than LLMs.</td></tr><tr><td>17.2 #2: unauthorized protected-book training issue</td><td><strong>Copyright infringement / intellectual-property and consent concerns — likely intended</strong></td><td><strong>Check wording:</strong> this identifies the controversy, not a legal finding about all AI training.</td></tr><tr><td>17.2 #3: Baudrillard&#x27;s copy/simulation term</td><td><strong>Simulacrum</strong></td><td>Singular; simulacra is plural.</td></tr><tr><td>17.3 #1: synthetic face/voice media</td><td><strong>Deepfake</strong></td><td>Deep-learning-based imitation/manipulation.</td></tr><tr><td>17.3 #2: reality and simulation blend</td><td><strong>Hyperreality</strong></td><td>Theoretical cultural condition.</td></tr><tr><td>17.3 #3: evaluate and verify digital information</td><td><strong>Digital literacy / media and information literacy</strong></td><td>The summary emphasizes critical media literacy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}], "checks": [{"id": "13.1-1", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.1", "number": 1, "question": "Smell imagery?", "answer": "Olfactory imagery", "note": "Olfactory = smell.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.1-2", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.1", "number": 2, "question": "Exaggerated comparison, not literal?", "answer": "Hyperbole — likely intended", "note": "Check wording: exaggeration points to hyperbole; a nonliteral comparison alone could be metaphor. The prompt mixes the definitions.", "ambiguous": true}, {"id": "13.1-3", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.1", "number": 3, "question": "Words imitate sounds?", "answer": "Onomatopoeia", "note": "Hiss and thud imitate sounds.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.2-1", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.2", "number": 1, "question": "Single rhythmic unit?", "answer": "Foot", "note": "A line is divided into feet.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.2-2", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.2", "number": 2, "question": "Da-DUM rhythm?", "answer": "Iamb / iambic rhythm", "note": "Pentameter additionally requires five feet.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.2-3", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.2", "number": 3, "question": "Marking stresses?", "answer": "Scansion", "note": "Analysis of meter and rhythm.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.3-1", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.3", "number": 1, "question": "Nonliteral comparison explaining an idea?", "answer": "Metaphor", "note": "Presents one thing as another.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.3-2", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.3", "number": 2, "question": "Concrete object represents abstract idea?", "answer": "Symbol", "note": "Object carries meaning beyond itself.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "13.3-3", "week": 13, "lesson": "13.3", "number": 3, "question": "Addressing “O, Death!”?", "answer": "Apostrophe", "note": "Direct address to an abstraction.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.1-1", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.1", "number": 1, "question": "Tragic error/flaw?", "answer": "Hamartia", "note": "The error contributing to downfall.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.1-2", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.1", "number": 2, "question": "Emotional release/purification?", "answer": "Catharsis", "note": "Audience response to tragedy.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.1-3", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.1", "number": 3, "question": "American playwright; common person's tragedy?", "answer": "Arthur Miller", "note": "Author of Death of a Salesman.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.2-1", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.2", "number": 1, "question": "Believable life on stage?", "answer": "Realism", "note": "Social and psychological plausibility.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.2-2", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.2", "number": 2, "question": "Imaginary audience barrier?", "answer": "Fourth wall", "note": "An invisible theatrical convention.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.2-3", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.2", "number": 3, "question": "Repetitive, illogical postwar theatre?", "answer": "Theatre of the Absurd", "note": "Beckett is the main example.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.3-1", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.3", "number": 1, "question": "Brecht's political theatrical style?", "answer": "Epic theatre", "note": "Encourages analysis of society.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.3-2", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.3", "number": 2, "question": "Reminds viewers this is a performance?", "answer": "Alienation / distancing effect", "note": "Disrupts passive immersion.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "14.3-3", "week": 14, "lesson": "14.3", "number": 3, "question": "1959 Black family's housing struggle?", "answer": "A Raisin in the Sun", "note": "Written by Lorraine Hansberry.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.1-1", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.1", "number": 1, "question": "Respect/care for parents and ancestors?", "answer": "Filial piety / Xiao", "note": "Family-centered ethical responsibility.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.1-2", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.1", "number": 2, "question": "Cosmic order, righteousness, sacred duty?", "answer": "Dharma", "note": "Distinct from karma/action.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.1-3", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.1", "number": 3, "question": "Confucius's sayings and dialogues?", "answer": "The Analects", "note": "Also called Lunyu.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.2-1", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.2", "number": 1, "question": "Lens challenging colonial legacies?", "answer": "Postcolonial criticism / postcolonial theory", "note": "The prompt asks for a critical lens.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.2-2", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.2", "number": 2, "question": "Europe as universal standard?", "answer": "Eurocentrism", "note": "Europe-centered assumptions.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.2-3", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.2", "number": 3, "question": "Achebe's 1958 novel?", "answer": "Things Fall Apart", "note": "Its title draws on Yeats's The Second Coming.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.3-1", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.3", "number": 1, "question": "Literal Noli translation?", "answer": "Touch me not", "note": "Title's Latin meaning.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.3-2", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.3", "number": 2, "question": "Corrupt friar archetype?", "answer": "Padre Damaso / Father Damaso", "note": "The character singled out in the lesson.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "15.3-3", "week": 15, "lesson": "15.3", "number": 3, "question": "Marginalized people's revolutionary spirit?", "answer": "Elias", "note": "Expected within the module's interpretation.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.1-1", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.1", "number": 1, "question": "Sudden profound realization?", "answer": "Epiphany", "note": "A meaningful change in understanding.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.1-2", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.1", "number": 2, "question": "Quiet unresolved ending?", "answer": "Chekhovian anti-climax", "note": "Module's phrase; “open ending” also describes it.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.1-3", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.1", "number": 3, "question": "Russian physician and story master?", "answer": "Anton Chekhov", "note": "Author of The Lady with the Dog.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.2-1", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.2", "number": 1, "question": "Brief personal account in an essay?", "answer": "Anecdote", "note": "A small story illustrates a wider argument.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.2-2", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.2", "number": 2, "question": "True accounts with literary techniques?", "answer": "Creative nonfiction", "note": "Fact-based literary storytelling.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.2-3", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.2", "number": 3, "question": "Shooting an Elephant author?", "answer": "George Orwell", "note": "Essayist and novelist.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.3-1", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.3", "number": 1, "question": "Thematic slice of a life?", "answer": "Memoir", "note": "Selective life writing.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "16.3-2", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.3", "number": 2, "question": "Implicit factual agreement?", "answer": "Truth contract / nonfiction pact", "note": "Check wording: no single technical label is explicitly supplied by the lesson. “Autobiographical pact” is related, but more specific to autobiography.", "ambiguous": true}, {"id": "16.3-3", "week": 16, "lesson": "16.3", "number": 3, "question": "Exposing others' private lives?", "answer": "Ethics of life writing / privacy dilemma", "note": "Check wording: the lesson gives a dilemma, not one uniquely established identification term.", "ambiguous": true}, {"id": "17.1-1", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.1", "number": 1, "question": "Narrative moves from print to film?", "answer": "Adaptation / cinematic adaptation", "note": "Most likely intended; the lesson also describes this through remediation.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.1-2", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.1", "number": 2, "question": "Shots, lighting, editing vocabulary?", "answer": "Cinematic language / visual grammar", "note": "“Cinematography” alone does not include all editing and sound choices.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.1-3", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.1", "number": 3, "question": "Modernizing text for new generation?", "answer": "Remediation — likely classroom intention", "note": "Check wording: “recontextualization” or “transformative adaptation” can be more precise; modernization alone does not require a medium change. No unique answer is established.", "ambiguous": true}, {"id": "17.2-1", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.2", "number": 1, "question": "AI generating content from prompts?", "answer": "Generative AI", "note": "Broader than LLMs.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.2-2", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.2", "number": 2, "question": "Unauthorized protected-book training issue?", "answer": "Copyright infringement / intellectual-property and consent concerns — likely intended", "note": "Check wording: this identifies the controversy, not a legal finding about all AI training.", "ambiguous": true}, {"id": "17.2-3", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.2", "number": 3, "question": "Baudrillard's copy/simulation term?", "answer": "Simulacrum", "note": "Singular; simulacra is plural.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.3-1", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.3", "number": 1, "question": "Synthetic face/voice media?", "answer": "Deepfake", "note": "Deep-learning-based imitation/manipulation.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.3-2", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.3", "number": 2, "question": "Reality and simulation blend?", "answer": "Hyperreality", "note": "Theoretical cultural condition.", "ambiguous": false}, {"id": "17.3-3", "week": 17, "lesson": "17.3", "number": 3, "question": "Evaluate and verify digital information?", "answer": "Digital literacy / media and information literacy", "note": "The summary emphasizes critical media literacy.", "ambiguous": false}], "works": [{"week": 13, "title": "Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening", "author": "Robert Frost", "summary": "A traveler pauses to watch snowy woods, then recalls obligations and continues. Attraction to stillness competes with responsibility; its steady meter supports that tension. The horse&#x27;s harness bells are an actual sound detail in this poem; the lesson&#x27;s apple imagery should not be assumed to come from this text.", "text": "A traveler pauses to watch snowy woods, then recalls obligations and continues. Attraction to stillness competes with responsibility; its steady meter supports that tension. The horse's harness bells are an actual sound detail in this poem; the lesson's apple imagery should not be assumed to come from this text."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Wild Geese", "author": "Mary Oliver", "summary": "Addresses guilt and loneliness by placing human life within an ongoing natural world. Nature&#x27;s sensory presence supports belonging and self-acceptance.", "text": "Addresses guilt and loneliness by placing human life within an ongoing natural world. Nature's sensory presence supports belonging and self-acceptance."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Ode to a Nightingale", "author": "John Keats", "summary": "A bird&#x27;s song prompts reflection on suffering, mortality, imagination, and escape. The speaker&#x27;s imaginative journey does not permanently remove human limitations.", "text": "A bird's song prompts reflection on suffering, mortality, imagination, and escape. The speaker's imaginative journey does not permanently remove human limitations."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Sonnet 18", "author": "William Shakespeare", "summary": "Compares the beloved with summer, then argues that poetry can preserve beauty against time. A Shakespearean sonnet has 14 lines, usually iambic pentameter, with the rhyme pattern ABAB CDCD EFEF GG.", "text": "Compares the beloved with summer, then argues that poetry can preserve beauty against time. A Shakespearean sonnet has 14 lines, usually iambic pentameter, with the rhyme pattern ABAB CDCD EFEF GG."}, {"week": 13, "title": "The Raven", "author": "Edgar Allan Poe", "summary": "A grieving speaker questions a mysterious raven and interprets its repeated answer through his loss of Lenore. Repetition and largely trochaic rhythm intensify obsession.", "text": "A grieving speaker questions a mysterious raven and interprets its repeated answer through his loss of Lenore. Repetition and largely trochaic rhythm intensify obsession."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Still I Rise", "author": "Maya Angelou", "summary": "A defiant speaker asserts dignity and resilience against oppression. Refrain, voice, and figurative language create confidence and momentum.", "text": "A defiant speaker asserts dignity and resilience against oppression. Refrain, voice, and figurative language create confidence and momentum."}, {"week": 13, "title": "My Life had stood", "author": "a Loaded Gun — Emily Dickinson", "summary": "A speaker imagines life as a gun brought into activity by an owner. Power, agency, dependence, violence, and mortality complicate the metaphor. Reducing it to loneliness alone misses major interpretive possibilities.", "text": "A speaker imagines life as a gun brought into activity by an owner. Power, agency, dependence, violence, and mortality complicate the metaphor. Reducing it to loneliness alone misses major interpretive possibilities."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Harlem", "author": "Langston Hughes", "summary": "Asks what happens when a dream is repeatedly postponed, testing unsettling sensory comparisons before suggesting possible eruption. Connect private frustration with blocked social opportunity.", "text": "Asks what happens when a dream is repeatedly postponed, testing unsettling sensory comparisons before suggesting possible eruption. Connect private frustration with blocked social opportunity."}, {"week": 13, "title": "Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair", "author": "Pablo Neruda", "summary": "A collection, not one poem. Explores desire, remembered intimacy, absence, and loss through bodily and natural imagery.", "text": "A collection, not one poem. Explores desire, remembered intimacy, absence, and loss through bodily and natural imagery."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Oedipus Rex", "author": "Sophocles", "summary": "Thebes suffers a plague. King Oedipus investigates the former king&#x27;s murder and discovers that he himself killed his father and married his mother, despite attempts to avoid the prophecy. Jocasta dies by suicide; Oedipus blinds himself. Connect investigation, ignorance, responsibility, and recognition.", "text": "Thebes suffers a plague. King Oedipus investigates the former king's murder and discovers that he himself killed his father and married his mother, despite attempts to avoid the prophecy. Jocasta dies by suicide; Oedipus blinds himself. Connect investigation, ignorance, responsibility, and recognition."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Macbeth", "author": "William Shakespeare", "summary": "After hearing a prophecy, Macbeth murders Duncan and takes the throne. Fear of losing power drives more violence; Lady Macbeth deteriorates, and Macbeth is ultimately killed. Ambition and choices matter alongside prophecy. <a href=\"https://www.rsc.org.uk/macbeth/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Royal Shakespeare Company synopsis ↗</a>.", "text": "After hearing a prophecy, Macbeth murders Duncan and takes the throne. Fear of losing power drives more violence; Lady Macbeth deteriorates, and Macbeth is ultimately killed. Ambition and choices matter alongside prophecy. [Royal Shakespeare Company synopsis](https://www.rsc.org.uk/macbeth/)."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Death of a Salesman", "author": "Arthur Miller", "summary": "Aging salesman Willy Loman clings to a dream of popularity and material success as his career and family relationships unravel. Biff challenges the family&#x27;s illusions. Willy dies by suicide, imagining his family will benefit financially. Connect dignity, self-deception, and the pressures of the American Dream.", "text": "Aging salesman Willy Loman clings to a dream of popularity and material success as his career and family relationships unravel. Biff challenges the family's illusions. Willy dies by suicide, imagining his family will benefit financially. Connect dignity, self-deception, and the pressures of the American Dream."}, {"week": 14, "title": "A Doll's House", "author": "Henrik Ibsen", "summary": "Nora secretly borrowed money and forged a signature to help her husband. When the truth emerges, Torvald values reputation over her sacrifice. Nora realizes the inequality of their marriage and leaves to seek an independent understanding of herself. Realism exposes domestic power.", "text": "Nora secretly borrowed money and forged a signature to help her husband. When the truth emerges, Torvald values reputation over her sacrifice. Nora realizes the inequality of their marriage and leaves to seek an independent understanding of herself. Realism exposes domestic power."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Waiting for Godot", "author": "Samuel Beckett", "summary": "Vladimir and Estragon wait near a tree for Godot. Encounters, repeated conversations, and promised arrivals do not resolve their waiting. Godot never appears. Connect repetition, uncertainty, dependency, and the search for meaning.", "text": "Vladimir and Estragon wait near a tree for Godot. Encounters, repeated conversations, and promised arrivals do not resolve their waiting. Godot never appears. Connect repetition, uncertainty, dependency, and the search for meaning."}, {"week": 14, "title": "The Cherry Orchard", "author": "Anton Chekhov", "summary": "A landowning family returns to an estate facing sale. They cannot adapt decisively; Lopakhin buys it, and the orchard is cut down. Private loss intersects with class and historical change.", "text": "A landowning family returns to an estate facing sale. They cannot adapt decisively; Lopakhin buys it, and the orchard is cut down. Private loss intersects with class and historical change."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Mother Courage and Her Children", "author": "Bertolt Brecht", "summary": "A trader follows armies during the Thirty Years&#x27; War, trying to survive and profit. War ultimately takes her three children, yet she continues with her cart. Ask how an economic system entangles survival with destruction.", "text": "A trader follows armies during the Thirty Years' War, trying to survive and profit. War ultimately takes her three children, yet she continues with her cart. Ask how an economic system entangles survival with destruction."}, {"week": 14, "title": "A Raisin in the Sun", "author": "Lorraine Hansberry", "summary": "The Younger family debates how to use an insurance payment: a home, a business, and education represent competing hopes. After financial loss and racist pressure, they choose to move into a white neighborhood. Connect dignity, dreams, and housing exclusion.", "text": "The Younger family debates how to use an insurance payment: a home, a business, and education represent competing hopes. After financial loss and racist pressure, they choose to move into a white neighborhood. Connect dignity, dreams, and housing exclusion."}, {"week": 14, "title": "Theatre of the Oppressed", "author": "Augusto Boal", "summary": "A work of theatre theory and practice, not a single plot-based play. Explores participatory performance through which people analyze oppression and rehearse possibilities for action.", "text": "A work of theatre theory and practice, not a single plot-based play. Explores participatory performance through which people analyze oppression and rehearse possibilities for action."}, {"week": 15, "title": "The Analects", "author": "Confucius and his followers", "summary": "Sayings and dialogues examine ethical cultivation, relationships, education, ritual, and leadership by example. Learn ren, xiao, li, and junzi rather than looking for one continuous plot.", "text": "Sayings and dialogues examine ethical cultivation, relationships, education, ritual, and leadership by example. Learn ren, xiao, li, and junzi rather than looking for one continuous plot."}, {"week": 15, "title": "Bhagavad Gita", "author": "traditionally attributed to Vyasa within the Mahabharata", "summary": "Arjuna&#x27;s battlefield crisis becomes a dialogue with Krishna about action, devotion, knowledge, and spiritual freedom. The problem is how to act rightly amid painful obligations.", "text": "Arjuna's battlefield crisis becomes a dialogue with Krishna about action, devotion, knowledge, and spiritual freedom. The problem is how to act rightly amid painful obligations."}, {"week": 15, "title": "Things Fall Apart", "author": "Chinua Achebe", "summary": "Okonkwo builds status while fearing weakness. He participates in Ikemefuna&#x27;s killing, later enters exile after an accidental death, and returns to a community transformed by missionaries and colonial institutions. His attempt at violent resistance fails to rally the community; he dies by suicide. The ending exposes the colonial official&#x27;s reduction of a complex life to a tiny anecdote. <a href=\"https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/things/summary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Supplementary plot reference ↗</a>.", "text": "Okonkwo builds status while fearing weakness. He participates in Ikemefuna's killing, later enters exile after an accidental death, and returns to a community transformed by missionaries and colonial institutions. His attempt at violent resistance fails to rally the community; he dies by suicide. The ending exposes the colonial official's reduction of a complex life to a tiny anecdote. [Supplementary plot reference](https://www.sparknotes.com/lit/things/summary/)."}, {"week": 15, "title": "Heart of Darkness", "author": "Joseph Conrad", "summary": "Marlow travels into the Congo to find the ivory trader Kurtz and encounters colonial brutality. Useful alongside Achebe: criticizing imperial violence does not necessarily prevent a text from marginalizing African voices.", "text": "Marlow travels into the Congo to find the ivory trader Kurtz and encounters colonial brutality. Useful alongside Achebe: criticizing imperial violence does not necessarily prevent a text from marginalizing African voices."}, {"week": 15, "title": "Noli Me Tangere", "author": "Jose Rizal", "summary": "Ibarra returns from Europe hoping to marry Maria Clara and build a school. Friar power and political intrigue undermine reform; he is falsely implicated in rebellion. Elias helps his escape and is fatally wounded. Maria Clara enters a convent, while the suffering of Sisa and her children reveals injustice beyond elite society. <a href=\"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6737\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Free English text: The Social Cancer ↗</a>.", "text": "Ibarra returns from Europe hoping to marry Maria Clara and build a school. Friar power and political intrigue undermine reform; he is falsely implicated in rebellion. Elias helps his escape and is fatally wounded. Maria Clara enters a convent, while the suffering of Sisa and her children reveals injustice beyond elite society. [Free English text: The Social Cancer](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6737)."}, {"week": 15, "title": "The Miseducation of the Filipino", "author": "Renato Constantino", "summary": "An essay criticizing colonial education for shaping Filipinos to see their society through foreign interests and assumptions. Connect education, language, identity, and national needs.", "text": "An essay criticizing colonial education for shaping Filipinos to see their society through foreign interests and assumptions. Connect education, language, identity, and national needs."}, {"week": 16, "title": "The Lady with the Dog", "author": "Anton Chekhov", "summary": "Married vacationers Dmitri Gurov and Anna Sergeyevna begin an affair in Yalta. Gurov expects something temporary but discovers enduring attachment. They reunite secretly and recognize the division between their public lives and private feelings. The ending leaves their practical future unresolved: realization has occurred, but no easy solution follows.", "text": "Married vacationers Dmitri Gurov and Anna Sergeyevna begin an affair in Yalta. Gurov expects something temporary but discovers enduring attachment. They reunite secretly and recognize the division between their public lives and private feelings. The ending leaves their practical future unresolved: realization has occurred, but no easy solution follows."}, {"week": 16, "title": "Hills Like White Elephants", "author": "Ernest Hemingway", "summary": "At a Spanish railway station, a couple discusses an unnamed operation, generally understood as abortion. Evasion, unequal pressure, and landscape imagery reveal conflict beneath apparently simple conversation. Connect implication, subtext, and unresolved choice.", "text": "At a Spanish railway station, a couple discusses an unnamed operation, generally understood as abortion. Evasion, unequal pressure, and landscape imagery reveal conflict beneath apparently simple conversation. Connect implication, subtext, and unresolved choice."}, {"week": 16, "title": "Shooting an Elephant", "author": "George Orwell", "summary": "A colonial officer investigates an elephant that has caused damage and killed a man. When he finds it calmer, he believes killing is unnecessary, but shoots under the pressure of spectators and fear of humiliation. Its painful death exposes the moral damage of performing imperial authority. <a href=\"https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/essays-and-other-works/shooting-an-elephant/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Original essay: Orwell Foundation ↗</a>.", "text": "A colonial officer investigates an elephant that has caused damage and killed a man. When he finds it calmer, he believes killing is unnecessary, but shoots under the pressure of spectators and fear of humiliation. Its painful death exposes the moral damage of performing imperial authority. [Original essay: Orwell Foundation](https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/essays-and-other-works/shooting-an-elephant/)."}, {"week": 16, "title": "Notes of a Native Son", "author": "James Baldwin", "summary": "Both a collection and the title of an essay. The title essay brings together Baldwin&#x27;s father&#x27;s death, racial conflict, anger, and reflection on inheritance. Personal experience becomes social criticism.", "text": "Both a collection and the title of an essay. The title essay brings together Baldwin's father's death, racial conflict, anger, and reflection on inheritance. Personal experience becomes social criticism."}, {"week": 16, "title": "The Year of Magical Thinking", "author": "Joan Didion", "summary": "Didion reflects on her husband&#x27;s sudden death and her daughter&#x27;s serious illness. Grief produces recurring memories and irrational hopes; the book explores how the mind struggles to accept loss.", "text": "Didion reflects on her husband's sudden death and her daughter's serious illness. Grief produces recurring memories and irrational hopes; the book explores how the mind struggles to accept loss."}, {"week": 16, "title": "I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings", "author": "Maya Angelou", "summary": "Recounts childhood and adolescence shaped by racism, sexual trauma, family relationships, and the discovery of voice and literature. Connect personal memory with social structures and resilience.", "text": "Recounts childhood and adolescence shaped by racism, sexual trauma, family relationships, and the discovery of voice and literature. Connect personal memory with social structures and resilience."}, {"week": 16, "title": "The Woman Warrior", "author": "Maxine Hong Kingston", "summary": "Blends autobiographical memory, family stories, and Chinese legend to explore Chinese American identity, gender, and voice. Its mixed forms invite careful questions about memory, cultural storytelling, and genre.", "text": "Blends autobiographical memory, family stories, and Chinese legend to explore Chinese American identity, gender, and voice. Its mixed forms invite careful questions about memory, cultural storytelling, and genre."}, {"week": 17, "title": "Frankenstein", "author": "Mary Shelley", "summary": "Victor Frankenstein creates a living being and abandons it. Rejection and revenge draw creator and creature into mutual destruction. Connect responsibility, ambition, exclusion, and how film versions reshape a novel.", "text": "Victor Frankenstein creates a living being and abandons it. Rejection and revenge draw creator and creature into mutual destruction. Connect responsibility, ambition, exclusion, and how film versions reshape a novel."}, {"week": 17, "title": "A Theory of Adaptation", "author": "Linda Hutcheon", "summary": "Treats adaptation as creative reinterpretation across forms and contexts, rather than evaluating it only as a faithful or unfaithful copy.", "text": "Treats adaptation as creative reinterpretation across forms and contexts, rather than evaluating it only as a faithful or unfaithful copy."}, {"week": 17, "title": "Literature and Film", "author": "Robert Stam", "summary": "Examines relationships between literary and cinematic storytelling. Use it to think beyond the idea that every change from a book is a failure.", "text": "Examines relationships between literary and cinematic storytelling. Use it to think beyond the idea that every change from a book is a failure."}, {"week": 17, "title": "The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction", "author": "Walter Benjamin", "summary": "Examines how technologies of reproduction change art&#x27;s authority, uniqueness, reception, and political possibilities. Connect reproduction with the concept of aura.", "text": "Examines how technologies of reproduction change art's authority, uniqueness, reception, and political possibilities. Connect reproduction with the concept of aura."}, {"week": 17, "title": "Simulacra and Simulation", "author": "Jean Baudrillard", "summary": "Examines how signs and models can structure what people take to be reality. Connect simulacra with hyperreality; this is theory, not a deepfake-detection manual.", "text": "Examines how signs and models can structure what people take to be reality. Connect simulacra with hyperreality; this is theory, not a deepfake-detection manual."}, {"week": 17, "title": "21 Lessons for the 21st Century", "author": "Yuval Noah Harari", "summary": "Essays examine contemporary technological, political, and social challenges, including how people understand information and uncertainty.", "text": "Essays examine contemporary technological, political, and social challenges, including how people understand information and uncertainty."}, {"week": 17, "title": "The Age of Surveillance Capitalism", "author": "Shoshana Zuboff", "summary": "Analyzes commercial systems that extract behavioral data and use it to predict and influence conduct. Connect digital power with the economics behind online information.", "text": "Analyzes commercial systems that extract behavioral data and use it to predict and influence conduct. Connect digital power with the economics behind online information."}]};